      "hints": [
        "Use $\\tan^2\\theta + 1 = \\sec^2\\theta \\implies (\\tan^2\\theta - 1)\\cos^2\\theta = \\sin^2\\theta - \\cos^2\\theta$.",
        "Substitute $\\cos^2\\theta = 1 - \\sin^2\\theta \\implies 2\\sin^2\\theta - 1$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Use the identity $\\tan^2\\theta + 1 = \\sec^2\\theta = \\frac{1}{\\cos^2\\theta}$ to rewrite the quotient:\n$$\\frac{\\tan^2\\theta - 1}{\\tan^2\\theta + 1} = \\frac{\\tan^2\\theta - 1}{\\sec^2\\theta} = (\\tan^2\\theta - 1)\\cos^2\\theta$$",
        "Multiply out the bracket using $\\tan\\theta = \\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}$:\n$$\\left(\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos^2\\theta} - 1\\right)\\cos^2\\theta = \\sin^2\\theta - \\cos^2\\theta$$",
        "Substitute $\\cos^2\\theta = 1 - \\sin^2\\theta$ to express in terms of $\\sin^2\\theta$:\n$$\\sin^2\\theta - (1 - \\sin^2\\theta) = 2\\sin^2\\theta - 1 \\quad (a = 2, \\, b = -1)$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
        "mode": "ai",
        "ai_rubric": {
          "Trigonometric Identities": "Uses $\\tan^2\\theta+1 = \\sec^2\\theta$ (M1). Expands to $\\sin^2\\theta - \\cos^2\\theta$ (A1). Obtains $2\\sin^2\\theta - 1$ (A1)."
        },
        "criteria_source": "mark_scheme_structured"
